s the model author's convention | observed | LH3 (D10) |\n123\t| H15 | `assertion_admission.py:146-148`, `:188-191`; include-root-routes plan §1 H11 | the admission snapshot copies one model file, so an include root fails its include read. `gnd judgments check` accepts include roots since IR1 | observed | LH3 |\n124\t| H16 | `assertion_admission.py:35-39`, `:83-88`, `:118`, `:132` | checks that belong to the packaged shape only: the five closed tag vocabularies, the rule that `conditional` needs a condition and `unqualified` forbids one, and the fixed `covers` list | observed | LH3 |\n125\t| H17 | consumer of `assertion_admission.py` | `lineage_run.py:33` imports `ABSENT_CONDITIONS`, `PASSAGE`, `ROLES`, `SCHEMA`, `STATEMENT`, `WORK` and `admit`; `:203` calls `admit` with six positional arguments; `:254` compares the marker's `schema` with `SCHEMA` | observed | LH3, LH4 |\n126\t| H18 | probe, 2026-11-22 | packaged admission output at `4ad0d216`: `admitted.population.json` `6e45b93d…39e1`, `raw.population.json` `ea96584c…0173`, `judgment-report.json` `3726a424…c43c`, `admission.json` `6d262018…f16f`. Statement indices 3, 4, 5 and 6 pass `semantic-support` and fail `positive-own-findings` with `negated`, `baseline-comparison`, `speculation`, `cites-other-work` | observed | LH3, LH4 |\n127\t| H19 | #215, measured shape | 98 % of claims share a passage; about half have other than one actor and one object; 9 % have several conditions; 44 % carry a quantity | inherited | LH3 fixture |\n128\t| H20 | literature-depth close, row \"Host-scale reading and extraction\" | interface half only; the corpus and provider-budget run stays with the literature-identity follow-up | inherited | LH1, LH2 |\n129\t| H21 | PROJECT.md, roadmap after 0.4.0 | third milestone; independent of the two export milestones, both merged | inherited | all |\n130\t| H22 | component index, `scripts/components_index.py` | `examples.literature_reading` and `examples.literature_assertion_admission` exist, both `canonical`; the capabilities below adapt them and add no component. `review` lists seven literature entries whose descriptions are older than their code. The index changes in the same commit as the code | observed | all |\n131\t| H23 | probe, 2026-11-22; `reading.py:283-285`, `:393` | the loop completes only when `verify` and `check` both exit 0 on the broad and the targeted population, and admits only exits 0 and 1 from them. On a copy of the reading model, a population with one `system` fact and no `subject_label` exits 2 (`inadequate`) from both; adding one `subject_label` row gives 0 and 0. `LABEL` is what keeps the packaged model's one constrained fact type populated. A caller model with a constraint over a fact type a pass has not read yet exits 2, and the run refuses \"check exited 2\" | observed | LH1 (D16) |\n132\t\n133\tOut of scope: the corpus and provider-budget run (H20); promoting either\n134\tmodule to stable CLI; any change to a stable command or its caps (R0.7, H13);\n135\tthe assurance scorer's own 128-row cap (H10); #218, #221 and #222; release,\n136\ttag and publication.\n137\t\n138\t### Issue requests and the phases that discharge them\n139\t\n140\t| Request | Phase |\n141\t|---|---|\n142\t| #216: a public producer protocol, live and replayable | LH1, LH2 |\n143\t| #216: the stop rule per document as well as global | LH1 |\n144\t| #216: the roster cap and the byte budget as parameters | LH1 |\n145\t| #216: `replay()` against a caller-given input root | LH2 |\n146\t| #215: caller-named assertion entity, identity from its preferred uniqueness | LH3 |\n147\t| #215: every fact row of an admitted assertion kept | LH3 |\n148\t| #215: optional roles are optional | LH3 |\n149\t| #215: polarity and attribution rules as data, with a distinct disposition | LH4 |\n150\t| #215: `MAX_TARGETS` as a parameter | LH4 |\n151\t\n152\tThis is a framework milestone. An experiment-claim census (`gnd coverage`)\n153\tcounts claims over a domain model; it says nothing about which arguments an\n154\texample function accepts, so none is read here. The baseline is the test run\n155\tabove and the probes H6, H11, H18 and H23.\n156\t\n157\t## 2. Contract proposal and product behavior\n158\t\n159\tExact names and signatures are fixed in each phase spec. What follows is the\n160\tcontract each spec must realise.\n161\t\n162\t### LH1 — a live producer over a caller root\n163\t\n164\t- **The protocol.** A producer is any object with\n165\t  `produce(call) -> dict`. `call` is a `ProducerCall`: the stage, the section\n166\t  ID, its source ID and version, its kind and span, the section's source bytes\n167\t  and their sha256, and the open questions that point at the section. The\n168\t  result is the response object the loop accepts today: `outcome` (one of\n169\t  `accepted`, `empty`, `refused`), `facts` and `proposal_keys`. The loop\n170\t  counts calls itself; a producer needs no `calls` attribute. `SavedProducer`\n171\t  is one implementation and keeps its pin and roster checks.\n172\t- **A response is untrusted input.** The loop validates each response as a\n173\t  bounded JSON value of the declared shape before it uses it (standing\n174\t  rule 1). A response that fails, or a producer that raises, refuses the run\n175\t  with nothing written (D7).\n176\t- **A caller root.** A root that holds `profile.json` is a caller root. The\n177\t  profile names the reading model file and its include files, and supplies\n178\t  what the loop takes from constants today: the facts added to every\n179\t  population, the producer's label, and the optional review, calibration,\n180\t  conflict and absence steps. A\n181\t  root without a profile is the packaged layout and keeps the packaged\n182\t  constants. A live producer over a root without a profile refuses.\n183\t- **Steps.** A step the profile does not declare is recorded `skipped`\n184\t  (R0.6). A declared step runs on the caller's files. A reviewer, if given,\n185\t  is called once per accepted response and must return a `supported` receipt;\n186\t  any other decision refuses the run, as a stale review does today. The\n187\t  reviewer is a check, not a filter (D3).\n188\t- **Order.** Inputs are validated, then the reading model is extracted, then\n189\t  the first producer call is made (H7, standing rule 3).\n190\t- **Route exits.** On a caller root the exits of `verify` and `check` over\n191\t  the broad and the targeted population are recorded as observed, 0, 1 or 2,\n192\t  and the run completes (D16, H23). The report shows the exits; it never\n193\t  calls a population verified. The packaged root keeps requiring 0.\n194\t- **Per document.** `document_budget` bounds the bytes read from each source.\n195\t  A document that reaches it has its remaining sections omitted; other\n196\t  documents continue. The report states a stop reason for each source, with\n197\t  the three reasons the global stop has. A document whose required questions\n198\t  are resolved still gets its other scheduled reads (D6, ruled `(o)`).\n199\t- **Limits.** The roster cap, the largest budget, the per-file input cap, the\n200\t  response cap and the route timeout are fields of one limits argument, each\n201\t  with a default equal to today's constant and a hard ceiling (D4, R0.8).\n202\t- **What a caller run writes.** The captured response table and the captured\n203\t  review receipts are artifacts of the run. Its manifest has format\n204\t  `literature-reading-run/2` and records the settings, the unavailable\n205\t  sections and each step's status (D5, R0.5). A packaged run with no new\n206\t  argument writes `literature-reading-run/1`, byte for byte (R0.2).\n207\t- In LH1, `replay()` refuses a `/2` run by name; LH2 replaces that refusal.\n208\t\n209\t### LH2 — replay of a live run\n210\t\n211\t- `replay(folder, fixture=ROOT)` reads the manifest, takes the settings and\n212\t  step statuses from it, reads the caller root through its profile, and\n213\t  re-derives every artifact. It has no producer, reviewer, budget or limits\n214\t  argument.\n215\t- It refuses a response table, receipt table, stage, payload or input whose\n216\t  bytes differ from what the manifest binds, and a manifest whose settings\n217\t  exceed a hard ceiling.\n218\t- A manifest binds a run internally. `replay(..., expected_evidence_sha256=)`\n219\t  compares the run with the digest `run()` returned, which the host keeps\n220\t  outside the folder; without it a consistently rewritten folder replays. The\n221\t  README says so. For the packaged run the independent producer pin keeps\n222\t  that role.\n223\t- `score_reading_coverage` gains the same `fixture` argument and passes it\n224\t  through. Its 128-row cap stays (out of scope).\n225\t\n226\t### LH3 — admission over a caller's assertion entity\n227\t\n228\t- **Shape.** The caller passes an assertion shape: the ID of an entity type\n229\t  in its model, and the entity types to follow from it (D17, ruled). With no shape,\n230\t  `admit()` runs the packaged shape and writes today's bytes.\n231\t- **Attached facts.** A fact type is attached when one of its roles is played\n232\t  by the assertion entity. A row belongs to the assertion its role value\n233\t  names. Every attached row of an admitted assertion is kept.\n234\t- **Followed facts `(o)`.** Admission follows links outward (D9, ruled). When\n235\t  a kept row names an instance of a followed entity type, the rows of the\n236\t  fact types that entity plays a role in are kept for that instance too, and\n237\t  the walk continues from them. Example: a claim's row names quantity `Q7`;\n238\t  `Q7`'s value row and unit row are kept. The walk stops at the shape's\n239\t  list (D17, ruled): an instance of an entity type the shape does not list is\n240\t  kept as a value in the row that names it, and its own facts are not copied.\n241\t  With an empty list nothing is followed. One limit holds beside the list: a\n242\t  row that names an assertion which was not admitted is never kept, whichever\n243\t  route reaches it.\n244\t- **Coverage.** The admitted population's `covers` lists the attached fact\n245\t  types and the fact types of the followed entity types. A fact type outside\n246\t  both is not copied.\n247\t- **Identity `(o)`.** The assertion's own handle is its identity (D10,\n248\t  ruled). The model's preferred uniqueness is enforced by the stable\n249\t  commands, not re-implemented (R0.7). No rule links an objectified fact\n250\t  type's rows to the entity (H14): a shape that names an entity which\n251\t  objectifies a fact type refuses, naming the objectification. The packaged\n252\t  model's `assertion` is such an entity, so the packaged example stays on the\n253\t  packaged path.\n254\t- **Judged unit.** Policy targets are rows attached directly to the\n255\t  assertion. An assertion is supported when at least one of its rows is a\n256\t  target and every target among its rows is admitted by\n257\t  `gnd judgments check` (D8). A target on any other row refuses.\n258\t- **Optional facts.** An assertion with no row in an attached fact type\n259\t  simply has none. No value is read as \"absent\". The tag vocabularies and the\n260\t  condition rule of H16 apply to the packaged shape only.\n261\t- **Include roots.** The snapshot copies each declared include beside the\n262\t  root, and the record binds each include's sha256 (H15).\n263\t- **Record.** A call with a shape writes `literature-assertion-admission/2`;\n264\t  the packaged call keeps `/1` (D12).\n265\t\n266\t### LH4 — admission rules as data\n267\t\n268\t- **Rules.** The caller passes admission rules: a list of requirements, each\n269\t  naming an attached fact type, a role and the admissible values. An\n270\t  assertion satisfies a requirement when it has at least one row of that fact\n271\t  type and every such row has an admissible value. The packaged pair\n272\t  `(\"asserts\", \"own-finding\")` becomes the default rules, and the comparison\n273\t  at `:122` is removed (R0.4).\n274\t- **Disposition.** In a `/2` record, a supported assertion that fails a\n275\t  requirement has status `policy-refused` and names the requirement; an\n276\t  unsupported one keeps `fail` (D2). The `/1` record keeps today's statuses,\n277\t  so it keeps its bytes.\n278\t- **A rule that names nothing refuses.** A requirement naming an unknown fact\n279\t  type or role refuses the call. It is never evaluated as \"no row matched\".\n280\t- **Target cap.** `max_targets` is an argument from 1 to 1,024. The ceiling\n281\t  is the stable command's (H13, D11).\n282\t\n283\t## 3. Dependencies and artifact homes\n284\t\n285\t| Phase | Needs | Homes it changes |\n286\t|---|---|---|\n287\t| LH1 | nothing | `src/gnd/examples/literature/reading.py`, `src/gnd/examples/literature/depth.py` (one keyword on `_command`), a new `tests/test_lh1_reading_host.py`, a new `tests/fixtures/lh1-reading/` (the caller root and the recorded packaged trees), `src/gnd/examples/literature/reading_fixture/README.md`, `dev/components/` |\n288\t| LH2 | LH1 | `reading.py`, `src/gnd/examples/literature/assurance_report.py` (one pass-through argument), a new `tests/test_lh2_reading_replay.py`, the same README, `docs/literature/commands.md`, `docs/release-notes.md`, `dev/components/` |\n289\t| LH3 | nothing | `src/gnd/examples/literature/assertion_admission.py`, a new `tests/test_lh3_admission_shape.py`, a new `tests/fixtures/lh3-admission/`, `src/gnd/examples/literature/README.md`, `dev/components/` |\n290\t| LH4 | LH3 | `assertion_admission.py`, a new `tests/test_lh4_admission_rules.py`, `tests/fixtures/lh3-admission/` (rules files), the same README, `docs/literature/commands.md`, `docs/release-notes.md`, `dev/components/` |\n291\t\n292\tOrder: LH1, LH2, LH3, LH4, one at a time on the branch\n293\t`claude/literature-host-interfaces`. The graph is acyclic. The two pairs share\n294\tno source file; they run in sequence so that `docs/literature/commands.md`,\n295\t`docs/release-notes.md` and `dev/components/` have one writer at a time. Start\n296\tcondition: `main` at `4ad0d216`, which holds IR1, so LH3's fixture can be an\n297\tinclude root.\n298\t\n299\tFixtures are test fixtures under `tests/fixtures/`, not packaged examples: a\n300\tpackaged model would join the inventory in `tests/test_mc1_guards.py:365-376`\n301\tand the wheel. Each phase authors its own; no phase needs another phase's\n302\toutput files.\n303\t\n304\t## 4. Phases\n305\t\n306\t| Phase | Criteria | Inherited residuals |\n307\t|---|---|---|\n308\t| LH1 | **LH1-C1** a test producer with no `calls` attribute drives six stages over the caller root (two sources, its own model and include, its own section and fact names, no `responses.json`, `producer-pin.json` or `reviews.json`); broad and targeted populations exit 0 from `verify` and `check`; no packaged section, fact or label name occurs in the run folder. **LH1-C2** each call's bytes equal the section's span and its sha256 equals the attempt row's; `producer_calls` equals the calls the test producer counted. **LH1-C3** the run folder holds the captured response table and the captured receipts, both bound by the manifest. **LH1-C4** with no step declared, each step's status is `skipped` and the report says `skipped`, not `unavailable` and not a number; with each step declared, it runs on the caller's files; a declared file that is missing refuses before any producer call. **LH1-C5** a document budget ends one document and not the other; each source has its stop reason. **LH1-C6** a lowered roster cap and budget ceiling are honoured and recorded; a raised input cap admits a 200 KiB source the default refuses; a limit above its ceiling refuses. **LH1-C7** each malformed response in the spec's list refuses with `ExampleError` and nothing written. **LH1-C8** a producer that raises refuses, naming the stage and section. **LH1-C9** the three packaged scenarios equal the trees recorded at `4ad0d216`, through the version helper; `test_ld3`, `test_ld4` and `test_ld5` pass unedited. **LH1-C10** inputs at each roster ceiling load within the spec's time bound. **LH1-C11** a model `gnd extract` refuses, and a live producer over a root with no profile, each refuse before any producer call. **LH1-C12** `replay()` of a `/2` run exits 3. **LH1-C13** a caller run whose broad population leaves a constrained fact type empty completes and records exit 2 for it; one whose targeted population violates a constraint completes and records exit 1 | H20 stays carried |\n309\t| LH2 | **LH2-C1** the LH1-C1 run replays from its folder and the caller root, byte-identical, with the same evidence digest and zero calls. **LH2-C2** one changed byte in the response table or in a receipt refuses; a consistently rewritten folder refuses when the expected digest is given. **LH2-C3** a run recorded with non-default limits replays with them; a manifest with a setting above its ceiling refuses. **LH2-C4** a changed source, profile, model or include refuses \"stale reading input\". **LH2-C5** a `/1` run replays as today; `test_ld3`, `test_ld4`, `test_ld5` pass unedited. **LH2-C6** `score_reading_coverage` scores the caller run when given its root. **LH2-C7** a manifest that claims `skipped` for a step the profile declares refuses | none |\n310\t| LH3 | **LH3-C1** a model with claims that share a passage, a claim with two actors and no object, one with three conditions and one with a quantity that is an entity with a value row and a unit row `(o)` admits; every attached row of each admitted claim is kept, with the rows of its quantity `(o)`, and no row of another claim is; `gnd check` on the admitted population exits 0. **LH3-C2** `(o)` a shape naming an entity that objectifies a fact type, the packaged `assertion` for one, exits 3 with a reason that names the objectification, and nothing is published. **LH3-C3** a claim with one admitted and one rejected target is not admitted; a claim with one admitted target and untargeted rows is, with all its rows; a claim with no target has disposition `missing`. **LH3-C4** a row between two assertions is kept only when both are admitted. **LH3-C5** the model as an include root admits and the record binds each include; an include edited without a new pin exits 3 with nothing published. **LH3-C6** each refusal in the spec's list exits 3 with a value-free reason and nothing published. **LH3-C7** the value `none` in a generic-shape row is an ordinary value. **LH3-C8** the packaged call writes `admitted.population.json` and `raw.population.json` with the H18 digests and the H18 stage table; `test_li4` and `test_li5` pass unedited. **LH3-C9** 1,024 targets with their attached and followed rows project within the spec's time bound. **LH3-C10** `(o)` a quantity named by an admitted and by a rejected claim keeps its own rows once, and the rejected claim's row that names it is not kept; a row reached through a followed entity that names a rejected claim is not kept; an entity type the shape does not list is not followed (D17) | H15 closes here |\n311\t| LH4 | **LH4-C1** two rules files over the same inputs admit two different sets, each stated in the fixture. **LH4-C2** a supported assertion that fails a requirement has status `policy-refused`; an unsupported one has `fail`. **LH4-C3** an assertion with one admissible and one inadmissible row of the named fact type is `policy-refused`; so is one with no row of it. **LH4-C4** a lowered `max_targets` refuses a policy over it; a value above 1,024 refuses the call. **LH4-C5** a requirement naming an unknown fact type or role, and each malformed rules document in the spec's list, exit 3 with nothing published. **LH4-C6** the packaged call keeps the H18 bytes and statuses | none |\n312\t\n313\tEach phase also runs the structure and inventory guards,\n314\t`uv run ruff check src tests tools`, an independent review of its diff, a\n315\trefactoring review and its record.\n316\t\n317\t## 5. Documentation, skill and compatibility surfaces\n318\t\n319\t- **One home each.** The reading protocol, the profile, the limits and the\n320\t  `/2` manifest are described in\n321\t  `src/gnd/examples/literature/reading_fixture/README.md`, which already\n322\t  holds the stage files and the coverage states. The assertion shape, the\n323\t  admission rules and the `/2` record are described in the admission section\n324\t  of `src/gnd/examples/literature/README.md`. `docs/literature/commands.md`\n325\t  gains one short \"drive it from your host\" passage per example that links to\n326\t  those two homes and restates nothing normative.\n327\t- **Release notes.** One entry per pair under the unreleased heading of\n328\t  `docs/release-notes.md`, written by LH2 and LH4.\n329\t- **Skills.** No packaged skill names these functions; none changes.\n330\t- **Compatibility.** `SavedProducer.produce` takes one call object where it\n331\t  took two names; `test_ld3` reaches it only through the loop and through a\n332\t  patch that accepts any arguments. `run()`, `replay()`, `admit()` and\n333\t  `project()` keep their present arguments and defaults. `/1` readers are not\n334\t  given `/2` documents by any packaged route.\n335\t- **Issue replies.** The close drafts a reply to #216 and to #215. Each says\n336\t  what was not done: the 1,024-target ceiling (H13), the corpus run (H20) and\n337\t  admission of an objectified fact type (D10).\n338\t  The owner posts them.\n339\t\n340\t## 6. Risks and required controls\n341\t\n342\t| Risk | Control |\n343\t|---|---|\n344\t| The generic projection admits rows the packaged example refused | LH3-C8 and LH4-C6 pin the packaged admitted set, the stage table and the output digests |\n345\t| Dropping the saved-response pin for live runs weakens the packaged replay | the pin and roster checks stay for `SavedProducer` (existing `test_ld3` cases); LH2-C2 binds a live run by digest |\n346\t| A caller root silently gets a packaged constant | the profile is the only source of caller bindings; LH1-C1 searches the run folder for packaged names |\n347\t| A skipped step reads as a passed one | R0.6; LH1-C4 and LH2-C7 |\n348\t| A provider answer crashes the loop after spend | LH1-C7, LH1-C8; the order rule and LH1-C11 put every input and model refusal before the first call |\n349\t| A completed caller run is read as a verified extraction | D16; the report carries the route exits, the README says a completed run is not a verified one, and LH1-C13 holds a run that completes with exits 2 and 1 |\n350\t| Following outward keeps a rejected assertion's row, or grows to most of the population | the limit in §2 \"Followed facts\" and D17; LH3-C10 holds a shared entity, a row reached the long way round and an unlisted entity type |\n351\t| The reporter's assertion is an objectified fact type after all | LH3-C2 refuses it by name, and the #215 reply states the ruling (D10), so the mismatch is reported, not silent |\n352\t| A raised cap turns a legal input into minutes of CPU | R0.8; LH1-C10, LH3-C9 |\n353\t| A rule quantified \"any\" or \"every\" passes on fixtures that cannot tell them apart | LH3-C3, LH3-C4 and LH4-C3 each hold the discriminating case |\n354\t| A host reads \"parameter\" as \"no limit\" for `MAX_TARGETS` | LH4-C4; the README and the #215 reply state the stable ceiling |\n355\t| A large caller vocabulary exceeds the route timeout ([#221](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/issues/221)) | the timeout is a limit (D4); a route that times out refuses naming the route |\n356\t| LH1 is larger than one phase | its spec states the size; if the estimate passes 400 source lines the spec proposes a split at its gate, with new IDs LH1a and LH1b |\n357\t| The packaged bytes pin moves at a version bump | the comparison uses `tests/support/recorded_version.py`, with `RECORDED_AT = \"0.4.0\"` |\n358\t\n359\t## 7. Out of scope\n360\t\n361\tThe corpus and provider-budget run; stable-CLI promotion of either module; a\n362\tchange to `gnd judgments check`'s caps or to any stable command; batching\n363\tinside `admit()`; the assurance scorer's row cap; admission of an entity that\n364\tobjectifies a fact type under a shape (D10, ruled); following an entity type\n365\tthe shape does not list (D17, ruled); #218, #221, #222;\n366\trelease, tag and publication.\n367\t\n368\t## 8. Execution, approval and review protocol\n369\t\n370\t- The plan review is held to the project's bar, the maintained GND review\n371\t  panel. Which instrument runs, and whether it runs, is the owner's selection\n372\t  (D14). The two milestones before this one used the panel light, two rounds\n373\t  at most.\n374\t- The plan gate is recorded in `001-plan-gate.md` once the owner rules, with\n375\t  the review's instrument, reviewed digest and dispositions, or the statement\n376\t  that none ran.\n377\t- Execution is `process-milestone literature-host-interfaces`, or\n378\t  `process-phase LH1 literature-host-interfaces` for one phase. `auto=true`\n379\t  needs the owner's explicit delegation for this milestone; none has been\n380\t  given (D15).\n381\t- Each phase: spec, spec gate, code, focused gates, one independent reviewer\n382\t  over the diff (D13), refactoring review, record, commit.\n383\t- One pull request from `claude/literature-host-interfaces` to `main`. Before\n384\t  it, `python3 scripts/components_index.py changed --base origin/main\n385\t  --strict` exits 0. Merge stays with the owner.\n386\t\n387\t## 9. Per-phase verification\n388\t\n389\t| Phase | Focused lane | Expected |\n390\t|---|---|---|\n391\t| LH1 | `uv run pytest -q tests/test_lh1_reading_host.py tests/test_ld3_reading_workflow.py tests/test_ld4_revision.py tests/test_ld5_verdict.py tests/test_li1_assurance_report.py --tb=short` | exit 0 |\n392\t| LH2 | `uv run pytest -q tests/test_lh2_reading_replay.py tests/test_lh1_reading_host.py tests/test_ld3_reading_workflow.py tests/test_ld4_revision.py tests/test_ld5_verdict.py tests/test_li1_assurance_report.py --tb=short`, then `uv run python tools/run_tutorials.py --group literature` | exit 0 each |\n393\t| LH3 | `uv run pytest -q tests/test_lh3_admission_shape.py tests/test_li4_assertions.py tests/test_li5_lineage.py --tb=short` | exit 0 |\n394\t| LH4 | `uv run pytest -q tests/test_lh4_admission_rules.py tests/test_lh3_admission_shape.py tests/test_li4_assertions.py tests/test_li5_lineage.py --tb=short`, then `uv run python tools/run_tutorials.py --group literature` | exit 0 each |\n395\t| all | the structure and inventory guard block of `project.md`, and `uv run ruff check src tests tools` | exit 0 |\n396\t\n397\tAffected consumers named here are read at each spec and may be widened there.\n398\tNo broad lane (`-m \"evidence or census\"`) is selected.\n399\t\n400\t## 10. Owner decisions and open questions\n401\t\n402\tD6, D9 and D10 carry the owner's ruling of 2026-11-22 `(o)`, and D17 the\n403\truling of 2026-11-23 `(o)`. Every other row is a proposal until the plan gate\n404\trules it. D1–D3 keep their outline IDs;\n405\tD4–D16 are new in r2 and D17 in r3.\n406\t\n407\t| ID | Question | Proposed | What the other reading costs |\n408\t|---|---|---|---|\n409\t| D1 | Where the producer protocol lives | In `reading.py`, until a second example uses it | A shared module now is a public home with one user |\n410\t| D2 | Name and reach of the LH4 disposition | `policy-refused`, in the `/2` record only; `/1` keeps `fail` | Applying it to `/1` changes the packaged record's bytes and `test_li4`'s expectations |\n411\t| D3 | The fixture-only steps on a caller root | Calibration, the conflict check and the absence scope are optional and declared in the profile; an undeclared step is `skipped`. The population label is profile data. A reviewer is optional, and a decision other than `supported` refuses the run | A reviewer that filters instead needs a ninth coverage state, which changes the row format #216 asks to keep |\n412\t| D4 | Which caps become parameters, and their ceilings | Five: roster cap (default 128, ceiling 4,096), largest budget (1 MiB, 64 MiB), per-file input (128 KiB, 8 MiB), response (64 KiB, 1 MiB), route timeout (30 s, 600 s) | #216 names the first two only; with only those, a source over 128 KiB still refuses and a slow `gnd check` still stops the run at 30 s |\n413\t| D5 | Manifest format of a caller run | `literature-reading-run/2` for any run that uses a new argument; `/1` unchanged otherwise | Optional keys in `/1` let an older reader accept a run it cannot re-derive |\n414\t| D6 | What \"the stop rule per document\" means | **Ruled `(o)` 2026-11-22: as proposed.** A byte budget per source, enforced in the loop, and a stop reason per source in the report. A document whose required questions are resolved still gets its other scheduled reads | Ending a document at required coverage reads less, and changes which optional questions are answered; it could be a later switch |\n415\t| D7 | A malformed response, or a producer that raises | Refuse the run with nothing written; a host that prefers to continue returns `refused` from its own producer | Recording it as `refused` reports a transport failure as the producer's verdict |\n416\t| D8 | LH3: what is judged, and how row verdicts make an assertion's verdict | Targets are attached rows; supported means at least one target and every target admitted | \"Any target admitted\" admits an assertion with a rejected participant row |\n417\t| D9 | LH3: how far \"hangs off\" reaches | **Ruled `(o)` 2026-11-22: the other reading, follow entities outward.** Not adopted: directly attached fact types only; they form `covers`; other fact types are not copied | Following entities outward keeps a quantity entity's own facts, at the price of a reachability rule the model does not state. That rule is D17 |\n418\t| D10 | LH3: linking an objectified fact's row to its handle | **Ruled `(o)` 2026-11-22: the other reading, own handle only.** Not adopted: a link through the preferred external uniqueness, each identifying role matched to a role of the same name and object type in the objectified fact type | No rule means the objectified form is refused and only entities with their own handle are admitted, which leaves the packaged model on the old path only |\n419\t| D11 | LH4: the ceiling of `max_targets` | 1,024, the stable command's cap; a larger set is admitted in several calls the host makes | Raising `judgments.py`'s caps changes a stable command; that would be its own issue |\n420\t| D12 | LH3: the record of a shaped call | `literature-assertion-admission/2`; the packaged call keeps `/1` | One format with optional keys makes `lineage_run.py:254` accept records it was not written for |\n421\t| D13 | Phase diff reviewer | The review helper (`.claude/skills/process-phase/scripts/review_tool.py`, subject `phase`), one reviewer in a different tool | none; this is `project.md`'s default |\n422\t| D14 | Plan-review instrument | The owner selects: the GND panel, the panel light, or none | An unreviewed plan goes to its gate saying so |\n423\t| D15 | How the milestone runs | The owner selects: phase by phase with an owner gate on each spec, or `process-milestone … auto` | none |\n424\t| D16 | LH1: which `verify` and `check` exits let a caller run complete | All three are recorded and the run completes; the host gates on them | Requiring 0, as the packaged root does, refuses every run over a model with a constraint on a fact type not yet read (H23). Refusing on 1 only loses every producer call of a run whose extraction violates a constraint, which is a finding the host wants kept |\n425\t\n426\t| D17 | LH3: where following stops (opened by the D9 ruling) | **Ruled `(o)` 2026-11-23: as proposed.** The shape lists the entity types to follow, for example `quantity`; an instance of any other entity type is kept as a value in the row that names it, and its own facts are not copied | Not adopted: following every entity type. It needs no list, but one admitted claim then keeps its paper, the paper's authors, their other papers and so on: most of the population, whatever was judged. With an empty list the result is the reading D9 did not adopt |\n427\t\n428\tThe owner ruled D6, D9, D10 and D17 without waiting for the reporter. The\n429\treply to #215 states the three LH3 rulings, so the reporter can say if their\n430\tmodel does not fit them: an assertion is identified by its own handle,\n431\tadmission follows outward from it, and the host lists the entity types it\n432\tfollows.\n433\t\n434\t## 11. Rulings before the gate\n435\t\n436\t### 2026-11-22, r3\n437\t\n438\tOwner rulings given in the planning session, in reply to the three questions\n439\tr2 marked for the reporter. The owner's words: \"d6: proposed\", \"d9:\n440\talternative\", \"d10: own number\". Prior wording is r2's.\n441\t\n442\t| Tag | Site | Prior wording (r2) | Now |\n443\t|---|---|---|---|\n444\t| o | §10 D6; §2 LH1 \"Per document\" | proposal, \"To confirm with the reporter\" | ruled as proposed; no criterion changes |\n445\t| o | §10 D9; Goal LH3; §2 LH3 \"Attached facts\" | \"The admitted population holds exactly the attached rows of admitted assertions, and its `covers` lists the attached fact types (D9).\" | \"Followed facts\" and \"Coverage\" in §2; LH3-C1 widened; LH3-C10 added; D17 opened |\n446\t| o | §10 D10; Goal LH3; §2 LH3 \"Identity\" | \"For an entity that objectifies a fact type, a row of that fact type is linked to its handle through the entity's preferred external uniqueness, role by role (D10, H14). A row that links to no handle or to two refuses.\" | own handle only; an objectifying entity refuses under a shape |\n447\t| o | §4 LH3-C2 | \"the packaged model under a shape naming `assertion` admits exactly the statements whose target is admitted, each with its two link rows\" | the refusal of an objectifying entity |\n448\t| o | §7 | \"facts reachable from an assertion only through another entity (D9)\" | removed; two items added for D10 and D1